# WHAT IS NOT PINNED, AND WHY.
#
# Upstream v0.10.0 carries 415 files under its docs/ and its qa/ that this file
# has never named, and after reading them the answer is still no:
#
#   docs/decisions/  — upstream's own ADRs and CRDs (58 files at this tag);
#   docs/design/     — upstream's own PRDs and designs (11);
#   docs/tasks/      — upstream's own task table, one file per task (20);
#   qa/              — upstream's QA cases, its run-all.sh and its qa lib (326).
#
# Two of those paths moved at v0.10.0: QA's folder came out of docs/ to qa/ at the
# repository root, and the task table became docs/tasks/. docs/research/ is not on
# the list any more because upstream deleted it: its CRD 0029 took the researcher's
# answers out of the repository altogether, and the researcher now writes no file.
#
# Every one of them is upstream's record of upstream's own jobs. None holds a rule
# this port carries, skips or restates, which is the test a pinned line has to
# pass. Pinning them would report dozens of FAILED lines per pass that say nothing
# about this port, and a check nobody reads is worse than no check, because it
# looks like one.
#
# The same goes for package.json, tools/, preset/, .github/workflows/ and
# cordis.patch.yml: nothing there is ported, and porting.md's "did not port"
# table names each of them with its reason.
#
# Some of that unpinned material is still worth reading by hand when a question
# comes up. qa/ is where upstream's own checks live: this port has none, by design
# rule 6, so upstream's cases are the only place to see what a rule was meant to
# make true. And the full quotations behind principles.md's "What each kind of
# document holds" were in docs/research/document-types.md, which v0.10.0 deleted
# along with the rest of that folder — it is one command away in the clone:
#   git -C "$TMP/dsh-crew" show v0.9.0:docs/research/document-types.md
# All of that is a person's job, not a checksum's.
